import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
---

<GuideLayout
  title="How to Taste Coffee"
  description="Learn to identify coffee tasting notes, use the flavour wheel, and develop your palate. A practical guide to cupping and sensory evaluation for home brewers."
  headline="How to Taste Coffee"
  subtitle="You don't need a Q-Grader certificate to taste the difference between a washed Ethiopian and a natural Brazilian. Here's how to train your palate at home."
  heroAccent="tasting"
  readTime="9 min"
  category="Flavour"
>

<h2>Why Most People Can't Taste the Difference</h2>

<p>
  It's not because your palate is bad. Nobody taught you what to pay attention to. When you drink coffee, your brain processes dozens of flavour compounds at once and collapses them into a single impression: "good" or "not good." Tasting deliberately means <strong>slowing down and isolating those signals.</strong>
</p>

<p>
  The good news: your palate is already more sophisticated than you think. You can tell the difference between an apple and a pear. Between milk chocolate and dark chocolate. Coffee tasting just applies that same skill to a more complex matrix.
</p>

<h2>The Four Dimensions of Coffee Flavour</h2>

<p>Every cup of coffee can be described along four axes. Learning to evaluate each one separately is the core skill:</p>

<div class="dimension-grid">
  <div class="dim-card">
    <div class="dim-header">
      <div class="dim-number">01</div>
      <h3>Aroma</h3>
    </div>
    <p>What you smell before you sip, and after. Aroma carries more information than taste alone. Swirl the cup, inhale deeply. Common descriptors: floral, nutty, fruity, chocolatey, earthy.</p>
    <div class="dim-try">
      <strong>Try this:</strong> Smell your coffee before and after each sip. The retronasal aroma (what you smell after swallowing) often reveals notes you missed.
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="dim-card">
    <div class="dim-header">
      <div class="dim-number">02</div>
      <h3>Acidity</h3>
    </div>
    <p>Not sourness but brightness. Acidity is the lively, tangy quality that makes coffee interesting. Think of it like the difference between flat water and sparkling water. High acidity = bright and juicy. Low acidity = smooth and mellow.</p>
    <div class="dim-try">
      <strong>Try this:</strong> Compare a Kenyan coffee (high acidity, like biting into a citrus fruit) with a Sumatran (low acidity, heavy and earthy).
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="dim-card">
    <div class="dim-header">
      <div class="dim-number">03</div>
      <h3>Body</h3>
    </div>
    <p>The weight and texture of the coffee on your tongue. Does it feel like skim milk or heavy cream? Body is influenced by brew method, roast level, and bean variety. French press produces more body than a V60 because it doesn't filter oils.</p>
    <div class="dim-try">
      <strong>Try this:</strong> Brew the same coffee on a French press and a V60. The body difference is immediately obvious.
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="dim-card">
    <div class="dim-header">
      <div class="dim-number">04</div>
      <h3>Sweetness & Finish</h3>
    </div>
    <p>How pleasant the aftertaste is, and how long it lingers. Great coffee has a clean, sweet finish. Poor coffee leaves a dry, ashy, or metallic taste. The finish is often where you can really tell quality apart.</p>
    <div class="dim-try">
      <strong>Try this:</strong> After swallowing, wait 10 seconds. What do you taste? The best coffees leave something you actually want to chase with another sip.
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<h2>The Coffee Flavour Wheel</h2>

<p>
  The SCA (Specialty Coffee Association) flavour wheel is the standard vocabulary for describing coffee. It's organized from general (center) to specific (outer ring). You don't need to memorize it, but having a shared language makes it much easier to describe what you're tasting.
</p>

<div class="wheel-breakdown">
  <div class="wb-category">
    <div class="wb-dot wb-dot--fruity"></div>
    <div class="wb-body">
      <h3>Fruity</h3>
      <div class="wb-notes">Berry · Citrus · Stone fruit · Tropical · Dried fruit</div>
      <p>Common in light-roasted African coffees, especially washed Ethiopians and Kenyans. Think blueberry, lemon zest, peach.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="wb-category">
    <div class="wb-dot wb-dot--sweet"></div>
    <div class="wb-body">
      <h3>Sweet</h3>
      <div class="wb-notes">Chocolate · Caramel · Honey · Brown sugar · Vanilla</div>
      <p>The backbone of most Central and South American coffees. Colombian, Brazilian, and Guatemalan beans often live here.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="wb-category">
    <div class="wb-dot wb-dot--floral"></div>
    <div class="wb-body">
      <h3>Floral</h3>
      <div class="wb-notes">Jasmine · Lavender · Rose · Chamomile · Bergamot</div>
      <p>Delicate and aromatic. Most prominent in Ethiopian Yirgacheffe and some Panamas (Gesha variety). Often detected more in aroma than taste.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="wb-category">
    <div class="wb-dot wb-dot--nutty"></div>
    <div class="wb-body">
      <h3>Nutty / Cocoa</h3>
      <div class="wb-notes">Almond · Hazelnut · Peanut · Dark chocolate · Cocoa</div>
      <p>Common in medium to dark roasts, especially Brazilian naturals. Comforting and familiar, and often what people describe as "coffee-tasting coffee."</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="wb-category">
    <div class="wb-dot wb-dot--spicy"></div>
    <div class="wb-body">
      <h3>Spicy / Earthy</h3>
      <div class="wb-notes">Cinnamon · Clove · Cedar · Tobacco · Pepper</div>
      <p>Indonesian coffees (Sumatra, Sulawesi) and some Indian beans. Heavy, complex and polarizing: people either love or hate these profiles.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<h2>How to Cup Coffee at Home</h2>

<p>Cupping is the professional method for evaluating coffee. It's surprisingly easy to do at home, and it's the fastest way to develop your palate:</p>

<div class="cupping-steps">
  <div class="cup-step">
    <div class="cup-num">1</div>
    <div>
      <strong>Grind coarse:</strong> about 11g of coffee, ground coarser than you'd use for French press.
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="cup-step">
    <div class="cup-num">2</div>
    <div>
      <strong>Smell the dry grounds.</strong> This is the "fragrance." Write down your first impressions.
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="cup-step">
    <div class="cup-num">3</div>
    <div>
      <strong>Pour 200ml of 93°C water</strong> directly onto the grounds. Start a 4-minute timer.
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="cup-step">
    <div class="cup-num">4</div>
    <div>
      <strong>Break the crust.</strong> After 4 minutes, use a spoon to push the floating grounds aside. Lean in and smell. This is the "aroma" and it's often the most revealing moment.
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="cup-step">
    <div class="cup-num">5</div>
    <div>
      <strong>Skim</strong> the remaining grounds off the surface. Wait 5–8 minutes for the coffee to cool.
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="cup-step">
    <div class="cup-num">6</div>
    <div>
      <strong>Slurp loudly.</strong> Use a spoon to slurp the coffee. The air spreads it across your whole palate. Evaluate acidity, body, sweetness, and finish.
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="tip-callout">
  <div class="tip-icon">💡</div>
  <div>
    <strong>Compare, don't just taste.</strong> Cupping one coffee teaches you a little. Cupping two coffees side by side teaches you a lot, because differences jump out when you have a reference point. Try cupping an Ethiopian next to a Brazilian.
  </div>
</div>

<h2>Building Your Palate Over Time</h2>

<p>
  Like any skill, tasting improves with deliberate practice. Here's a realistic path:
</p>

<div class="progression-grid">
  <div class="prog-card">
    <div class="prog-badge prog-badge--1">Week 1–2</div>
    <h3>Learn to Notice</h3>
    <p>Just slow down. Before your first sip, smell the coffee. After each sip, pause. Ask yourself: do I like this? Is it bright or smooth? Heavy or light? You're not naming notes yet, just paying attention.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="prog-card">
    <div class="prog-badge prog-badge--2">Week 3–4</div>
    <h3>Start Comparing</h3>
    <p>Buy two single-origin coffees from different regions. Brew them the same way and taste side by side. You'll immediately notice how different they are. Write down what you notice, even vague impressions like "this one is fruitier."</p>
  </div>
  <div class="prog-card">
    <div class="prog-badge prog-badge--3">Month 2–3</div>
    <h3>Build Vocabulary</h3>
    <p>Start matching your impressions to the flavour wheel. "Fruity" becomes "citrusy" which becomes "lemon zest." Read the tasting notes on the bag after you've written your own and see how they compare.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="prog-card">
    <div class="prog-badge prog-badge--4">Month 3+</div>
    <h3>Identify Origins Blind</h3>
    <p>With enough logged brews, you'll start recognizing regional characteristics. Ethiopian florals, Kenyan brightness, Brazilian nuttiness. This is when tasting becomes genuinely fun.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2>The Role of a Brew Journal</h2>

<p>
  <strong>Your palate has terrible long-term memory.</strong> You might taste "blueberry and chocolate" in a Yirgacheffe today, but in three weeks you won't remember the details. A journal fixes this. It becomes your memory, and it's the fastest way to train your palate.
</p>

<p>
  After every brew, log the basic parameters (method, dose, grind) and write 2–3 tasting notes. It doesn't need to be long or precise. Over time, patterns emerge: you'll discover which origins, roast levels, and brew methods you actually prefer, based on what you wrote down.
</p>


<aside class="tool-promo">
  <a href="/tools/extraction-yield-calculator/">
    <span class="tp-label">Put a number on it</span>
    <span class="tp-desc">If a cup tastes off, extraction yield tells you which direction to move.</span>
  </a>
</aside>

</GuideLayout>

<style>
  /* ── Dimensions ── */
  .dimension-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    gap: 1.25rem;
    margin: 2rem 0;
  }

  .dim-card {
    background: white;
    border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
    border-radius: 16px;
    padding: 1.5rem;
  }

  .dim-header {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  .dim-number {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    color: var(--amber);
    background: rgba(196,123,48,0.1);
    border: 1px solid rgba(196,123,48,0.2);
    width: 28px;
    height: 28px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }

  .dim-card h3 {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    color: var(--text-dark);
    margin: 0 !important;
  }

  .dim-card p {
    font-size: 0.9rem !important;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem !important;
  }

  .dim-try {
    background: var(--cream-subtle);
    border-radius: 10px;
    padding: 0.875rem 1rem;
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .dim-try strong { color: var(--text-dark); }

  /* ── Flavour Wheel Breakdown ── */
  .wheel-breakdown {
    margin: 2rem 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1.25rem;
  }

  .wb-category {
    display: flex;
    gap: 1.25rem;
    align-items: flex-start;
    background: white;
    border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
    border-radius: 14px;
    padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
  }

  .wb-dot {
    width: 14px;
    height: 14px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    margin-top: 0.35rem;
  }

  .wb-dot--fruity  { background: #E85D6C; }
  .wb-dot--sweet   { background: #C47B30; }
  .wb-dot--floral  { background: #B784D1; }
  .wb-dot--nutty   { background: #8B6F47; }
  .wb-dot--spicy   { background: #5A7247; }

  .wb-body h3 {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 1rem;
    color: var(--text-dark);
    margin: 0 0 0.35rem !important;
  }

  .wb-notes {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    color: var(--amber);
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .wb-body p {
    font-size: 0.88rem !important;
    margin: 0 !important;
  }

  /* ── Cupping Steps ── */
  .cupping-steps {
    margin: 2rem 0;
    background: var(--dark);
    border-radius: 18px;
    padding: 2rem;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1.25rem;
  }

  .cup-step {
    display: flex;
    gap: 1rem;
    align-items: flex-start;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    color: rgba(250,247,242,0.7);
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .cup-step strong { color: var(--cream); }

  .cup-num {
    width: 28px;
    height: 28px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: rgba(196,123,48,0.15);
    border: 1px solid rgba(196,123,48,0.3);
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--amber);
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }

  /* ── Tip Callout ── */
  .tip-callout {
    display: flex;
    gap: 1rem;
    margin: 2rem 0;
    background: rgba(196,123,48,0.06);
    border: 1px solid rgba(196,123,48,0.15);
    border-radius: 14px;
    padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .tip-icon { font-size: 1.25rem; flex-shrink: 0; margin-top: 0.1rem; }
  .tip-callout strong { color: var(--text-dark); }

  /* ── Progression ── */
  .progression-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    gap: 1.25rem;
    margin: 2rem 0;
  }

  .prog-card {
    background: white;
    border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
    border-radius: 16px;
    padding: 1.5rem;
  }

  .prog-badge {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.65rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    padding: 0.2rem 0.6rem;
    border-radius: 100px;
    display: inline-block;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  .prog-badge--1 { background: rgba(196,123,48,0.1); color: var(--amber); border: 1px solid rgba(196,123,48,0.2); }
  .prog-badge--2 { background: rgba(107,58,31,0.1); color: var(--brown); border: 1px solid rgba(107,58,31,0.2); }
  .prog-badge--3 { background: rgba(22,163,74,0.08); color: #16a34a; border: 1px solid rgba(22,163,74,0.18); }
  .prog-badge--4 { background: rgba(183,130,56,0.1); color: #b78238; border: 1px solid rgba(183,130,56,0.2); }

  .prog-card h3 {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    color: var(--text-dark);
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem !important;
  }

  .prog-card p {
    font-size: 0.88rem !important;
    margin: 0 !important;
  }

  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .dimension-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .progression-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }

  .tool-promo { margin: 2.5rem 0; }

  .tool-promo a {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.3rem;
    padding: 1.2rem 1.4rem;
    background: rgba(196,123,48,0.07);
    border: 1px solid rgba(196,123,48,0.22);
    border-radius: 14px;
    transition: border-color 0.2s, background 0.2s;
  }

  .tool-promo a:hover { border-color: var(--amber); background: rgba(196,123,48,0.11); }
  .tool-promo a:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--amber); outline-offset: 2px; }

  .tool-promo .tp-label {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.68rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.11em;
    color: var(--amber);
  }

  .tool-promo .tp-desc { font-size: 0.92rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); }
</style>
